<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lycortas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Lycortas of Megalopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lycortas of Megalopolis was a statesman and general of the Achaean League in the early 2nd century BCE and the father of the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>. A close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a>, he led the league after Philopoemen was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a>, recovering Messene in 182 BCE. He defended the league's independence against growing Roman interference in its affairs.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Achaean society was dominated by landowning families, who supplied the generals, cavalry commanders and envoys; Polybius, the son of the federal general <b>Lycortas</b>, came from such a family in Megalopolis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Megalopolis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Messene, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.41,22.13&amp;year=-135" aria-label="Megalopolis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5103 817 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lycortas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5553 1087h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5553 1087h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.41,22.13&amp;year=-135">What was here in 135 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
